      "math_academy_id": "q-69482",
      "sequence_position": 7,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196443/assets/6f1af14d60c9b2353d3aa149c4f74ead097289fd2a619013f1506cb783b72ee2.png)\n\nThe vectors $\\text{a},$ $\\text{b},$ and $\\text{c}$ are shown in the diagram above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vector $a-b-c.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we use the triangle law of addition to find \n\n$$\na-b=a+(-b).\n$$\n\nThe resultant $a-b$ is the vector that joins the tail of $\\text{a}$ and the head of $-\\text{b}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196443/assets/40912329e0fa213a0bb8be143ed2d44441634ced3eaf378d76a69aa3167fe14c.png)\n\nNow, we use the triangle law of addition to find \n\n$$\n(\\text{a}-\\text{b})-\\text{c}=(\\text{a}-\\text{b})+(-\\text{c}).\n$$\n\nWe draw $-\\text{c}$ from the head of $\\text{a}-\\text{b}.$ So, we have the vector that goes from the tail of $\\text{a}$ to the head of $-\\text{c}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196443/assets/6cbd0e043e9de98da0044792d5be99df0e1a17eff3b9dc8b87093985c5a27f24.png)",
